<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="how-it-works">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Calculations &amp; Shifts</div>
    <h2>Understanding the 5-Hour Offset</h2>
    <p>
      To convert Greenwich Mean Time to Central Daylight Time, you must subtract 5 hours from the standard reference clock. The formula is written as follows:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>CDT = GMT - 5 Hours</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      During the summer, the United Kingdom and Europe are often on daylight saving (e.g. BST, UTC+1), which widens the difference between local UK time and Chicago time to 6 hours. However, the standard baseline difference between GMT (UTC+0) and CDT (UTC-5) remains exactly 5 hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      In winter, North America transitions back to <strong>Central Standard Time (CST, UTC-6)</strong>. At that point, the calculation shifts to a 6-hour difference:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>CST = GMT - 6 Hours</code>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Greenwich Mean Time</h3>
      <code>UTC+0 Baseline</code>
      <p>GMT is the solar time standard centered on the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London. It does not observe daylight saving shifts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Central Daylight Time</h3>
      <code>UTC-5 Offset</code>
      <p>CDT is active in summer. It advances the Central zone clock by 1 hour to maximize evening sunlight and reduce energy use.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Central Standard Time</h3>
      <code>UTC-6 Offset</code>
      <p>CST is active in winter. It represents the standard solar meridian of Central North America, running 6 hours behind GMT.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

