<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     UNIQUE EDUCATIONAL SECTIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="topics" class="reveal">
  <h2>Legal History and Economic Impact of the Central Zone</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>The Creation of Standardized Railroad Time</h3>
      <p>
        Prior to the late 19th century, cities and towns across Central North America determined local time by solar noon. This solar time-keeping led to thousands of distinct local clock variations, rendering railroad travel complex and prone to collisions. In 1883, major railroad networks agreed to establish Standard Time zones.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Central Zone meridian was set at 90 degrees west of Greenwich, establishing Central Standard Time (CST) as GMT-6. Over time, the federal government formalized this geographic standard via the Standard Time Act of 1918.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Daylight Saving Legislation &amp; The Energy Policy Act</h3>
      <p>
        Daylight Saving Time transitions became standardized across the US under the Uniform Time Act of 1966. However, the schedule observed today was updated by the Energy Policy Act of 2005. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Starting in 2007, Congress extended daylight saving time by 4 weeks to conserve oil and electricity. As a result, CDT (UTC-5) is active for roughly 65% of the calendar year, running from March to November.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>GMT to CDT Business Integration &amp; Logistics</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>Working with Central US Businesses</h3>
      <p>
        Because Chicago, Dallas, and Houston house major corporate headquarters, remote engineering operations, and manufacturing plants, coordinating hours with Greenwich Mean Time is essential.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standard business overlap occurs between 1:00 PM GMT and 5:00 PM GMT, which translates to 8:00 AM CDT and 12:00 PM CDT. These four hours represent the optimal window for real-time meetings and sprint syncs.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Software Deployments &amp; Maintenance Windows</h3>
      <p>
        DevOps teams managing international cloud infrastructure regularly coordinate systems in CDT. Because major financial exchanges (such as the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, CME) close in the afternoon, scheduled database migrations often target 9:00 PM CDT (which is 2:00 AM GMT the next calendar day).
      </p>
      <p>
        This prevents service degradation during peak North American trading and business hours.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

