<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="source-notes" class="source-notes reveal">
  <h2>Common Confusions: GMT, CDT, CST, and UTC Explained</h2>
  <p>
    Timezone abbreviations are easily confused. Let's break down the definitions and clarify the common terminology:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>CDT vs. CST</strong>: This is the most common scheduling point of confusion. Central Daylight Time (CDT) is UTC-5 and active in summer. Central Standard Time (CST) is UTC-6 and active in winter. If you schedule a meeting in July using "CST", you are technically referring to a timezone that is 1 hour behind the active local time of Chicago, which can cause missed appointments.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GMT vs. UTC</strong>: Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is a historic solar time standard, whereas Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) is the modern atomic baseline. Although computer servers prefer the term UTC, GMT remains the preferred civil time descriptor in the UK and Europe. For standard civil conversion, their offsets are identical (+0).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Central Time (CT)</strong>: "Central Time" is a general umbrella term representing both CDT and CST. It is best to use "CT" when the calendar date of the event is unknown or when discussing regional business hours generally, as the system automatically shifts between standard and daylight saving offsets.
    </li>
  </ul>

